00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.500
It's not because people
want to take less trips.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.460
But this is because there
is a policy in New York

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:29.820
that no taxi driver
can be driving

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.520
more than 12 hours in a row.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.590
So the city enforces that law.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.680
And then all the
taxi drivers decided

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.330
to do their shift
change at 5:00 PM.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:46.890
So everyone goes
back to their garages

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.200
and the city doesn't have taxis.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.870
And you may want to compare
what's the behavior with Uber

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:58.800
or other for hire vehicles that
don't have that regulation.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.550
Well, as you can
see, the red line,

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:02.200
they don't have that data.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:07.740
And the question is, how do we
use this public data to find

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.640
better incentives so we don't
have a problem when people

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.460
can't find taxis at that time.

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:15.630
This is 2015.

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:22.950
If you go to 2016,
you can see that Uber

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.230
and the other for hire
vehicles caught up

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.930
to what the taxes are
doing at the same time.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.550
In fact, at 5:00 PM,
you have this date.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.420
But you don't have
a date for Uber.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:39.020
And then in 2017, you can see
how they have surpassed it.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.070
They do way more trips than
their regular yellow cabs.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:46.120
And the taxi drivers still
have that gap at 5:00 PM.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:50.550
And this is on the side of
how we can drive utility out

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.110
of public data.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.880
On the other side,
on the other hand,

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.290
we start to see
this kind of news.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:04.820
Like, oh, there is a privacy
risk on publishing these laws.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.410
We can find out where people are
going, where celebrities went.

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:10.870
And the question
here is, how do we

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:15.950
find a balance between
not publishing this data--

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:18.460
that would be the best
way to preserve privacy--

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:22.000
and all the utility that we
can get as individuals or we

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.830
can get at the city level
from publishing this data.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:32.470
To find this balance, to reduce
risk when publishing data,

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:35.578
Scott is going to tell us
some of these techniques.

00:05:35.578 --> 00:05:36.870
SCOTT ELLIS: Thank you, Felipe.

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:40.350
Great.

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:44.100
And so Google has been
working with a lot

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.260
of different applications with
a lot of users for a long time.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.290
And so because of that,
we have done a lot

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.937
on the security and privacy
side to understand our data

00:05:51.937 --> 00:05:53.520
and to protect it
so that we can still

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.200
make it usable for valuable
services for our customers.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.700
And so we're going to share
some of the tools and techniques

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:03.240
that we've released for you
to use, as well, to do this.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.910
One of those tools is our
cloud data loss prevention

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:09.100
infrastructure, or what
we refer to as DLP.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.190
And this covers
three main things

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:13.230
that it will do that we're
going to cover today.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:16.150
It helps you discover and
classify sensitive data.

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:17.850
It helps you go in
and mask that data.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:19.350
Let's say once you
find it, it helps

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:22.980
you redact it, reduce
it, mask it, tokenize it,

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.160
things like that.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.460
And the third thing is
it helps you measure

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:28.535
statistical risk that's
still in the data

00:06:28.535 --> 00:06:29.910
or potentially
still in the data,

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:31.840
so you can understand
what's there.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.690
And we're going to
cover those today.

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:36.425
I'm just going to
give a quick overview.

00:06:36.425 --> 00:06:37.800
And just to get
started, we think

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.500
a lot about security,
privacy, and compliance.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.500
All this infrastructure has
gone through a lot of audits

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.873
to make sure that as
we're handling your data,

00:06:45.873 --> 00:06:47.040
you can check some of these.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.830
We actually have a security
page dedicated to compliance,

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:50.503
as well.

00:06:50.503 --> 00:06:52.170
And you'll see our
product listed there,

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:54.290
as well as many others.

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:55.650
Let's get into what it does.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.840
So the first main capability
that it helps us with

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.923
is discovery and classification
of sensitive data.

00:07:01.923 --> 00:07:03.840
So this is things like
Personally Identifiable

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.808
Information, PII, different
types of identifiers.

00:07:06.808 --> 00:07:08.850
And a lot of what we're
going to talk about today

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:12.060
is how data, when it's
tied to a human individual,

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:12.930
can be sensitive.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.870
So you release some number.

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:15.870
It's not that sensitive.

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:18.663
You tie it to an individual's
phone number or email address,

00:07:18.663 --> 00:07:20.580
all of a sudden, that
number is very sensitive

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:22.865
because I know who it's about.

00:07:22.865 --> 00:07:24.240
So to get started,
when you think

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:28.440
of direct identifiers,
things like PII,

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:29.940
first thing is discover it.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:31.750
Find it in your data.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:33.750
Out of the box, we have
about 90 different types

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:36.750
that we support that you can
simply go in, turn on, and look

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:40.408
for, both in the US, global,
and several other countries

00:07:40.408 --> 00:07:42.450
where we have unique
identifiers in that country.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.340
Next is what do you
do and you find it?

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.188
So there's a lot of techniques.

00:07:49.188 --> 00:07:50.730
We're going to cover
a few them here.

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:53.670
But you see in this animation
we're showing a structured data

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:54.728
table.

00:07:54.728 --> 00:07:55.770
And there's four columns.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:57.187
And I'll kind of
talk through what

00:07:57.187 --> 00:07:59.250
we're doing in each
column just as an example.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.800
So in that first column,
it's an identifier.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:04.690
We don't want to expose
that raw identifier,

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:08.550
so we are using a format
preserving encryption method

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:11.203
to create what we call
a deterministic token.

00:08:11.203 --> 00:08:13.620
So what's happening is we're
actually taking out the value

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:16.350
and replacing it with something
else that still retains

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:18.990
the referential integrity and
the statistical properties

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:20.033
of that data.

00:08:20.033 --> 00:08:22.200
That's key because if you
want to join on that data,

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.600
you want to run analytics,
you want to do a group by,

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:26.760
an average, you still can
do it, but you're just

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:29.310
not revealing that raw PII.

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:31.650
In the second
column, we are taking

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:34.110
some somewhat
distinguishing data

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:36.090
and we're making it
less distinguishing,

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:39.923
a term referred to as
generalization or bucketing.

00:08:39.923 --> 00:08:41.340
And the idea here,
again, is we're

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:44.085
going to take something like
a senior engineer or vice

00:08:44.085 --> 00:08:45.960
president and we're just
going to bucket them

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:48.120
into a general bucket
called engineer.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:50.593
In the third one, we're
doing just partial masking.

00:08:50.593 --> 00:08:51.760
It's pretty straightforward.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.960
In that fourth column, we're
combining our inspection

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:55.830
with our masking.

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:58.440
So we're actually hunting
through the unstructured data

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.328
in that column, finding the
element that's sensitive,

00:09:01.328 --> 00:09:02.620
and then doing something there.

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:04.720
So in this case, we've
redacted a few things

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.170
or applied that
partial masking again.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:09.870
And again, all this running
is sort of one operation.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:15.510
So the next part when we
talked about risk analysis

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:18.290
or re-identification risk, I'm
going to cover it a little bit.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:20.230
And then Felipe is going
to go in more depth.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:21.730
But just to give a
high level, let's

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.857
say you removed all the
PII out of your data, all

00:09:23.857 --> 00:09:25.440
the direct identifiers,
all the things

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:28.140
that you can think about maybe
that seem kind of obvious--

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:30.560
so someone's credit card
number, social security number,

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.920
you've masked it or you've
done something there.

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:34.587
But what about something
like job title?

00:09:34.587 --> 00:09:36.720
We kind of hinted at
it in the last slide.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:39.588
But even just looking at
these five rows of data,

00:09:39.588 --> 00:09:41.880
you'll see that there's four
software engineers and one

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:43.410
CEO of Google.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:45.060
So that metric that
I have next to it,

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:46.685
let's say it's a very
sensitive metric.

00:09:46.685 --> 00:09:50.490
Maybe it's rating that they have
or a donation that they made

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:52.740
or some financial or
medical information.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:54.430
If you were to release
this information,

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:57.870
anyone who sees this is
probably going to figure out

00:09:57.870 --> 00:09:59.630
who the CEO of Google is.

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:01.470
It might be one person.

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:03.940
If you look historically, it
could be two or three people.

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:05.640
But the idea is it's
a single individual

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:08.850
or a small group of people are
going to map to that value.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:11.340
And everyone kind
of knows who it is.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:12.840
Whereas if I said
software engineer

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:15.243
and I did an average of
those four values, even,

00:10:15.243 --> 00:10:17.160
you probably don't know
what I'm talking about

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:18.420
or who I'm talking about.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:20.130
You're just getting
a nice, usable metric

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:23.937
which is the average for
that software engineer.

00:10:23.937 --> 00:10:25.770
So when we talk about
re-identification risk

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:28.230
analysis, this helps us
identify and pull out some

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:31.047
of those high risk outliers.

00:10:31.047 --> 00:10:32.630
And let's just give
a couple examples.

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:34.010
Again, this is kind
of what the bucketing

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:35.490
thing looked like that we did.

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:39.350
We went in and took something
more distinguishing and bucket

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:40.670
it to be less distinguishing.

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:42.780
That's one way we would
solve that job title.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:46.400
We'd bucket maybe CEO
or VP or senior VP

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:48.650
or whatever these kind
of high risk outliers

00:10:48.650 --> 00:10:52.913
are into just one
bucket called executive.

00:10:52.913 --> 00:10:55.080
Another thing we do with
dates that's kind of unique

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.335
is an option called
date shifting.

00:10:57.335 --> 00:10:58.710
So in some cases,
the actual date

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:01.110
of a transaction or
the date somebody

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:04.360
was admitted into a hospital
can be sensitive by itself.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:06.943
But if you were to
redact it or blur it just

00:11:06.943 --> 00:11:08.610
to something like the
month or the year,

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.318
you start to lose some
granularity there.

00:11:10.318 --> 00:11:13.630
And it can be very hard to
analyze a sequence of events.

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:17.490
So in this just really simple
example, we've taken records

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:19.710
and we basically
generate a random shift

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:22.005
in the context of that person.

00:11:22.005 --> 00:11:24.630
So in this case, it's a medical
record number that we're using.

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:26.010
The idea is that we're
not going to shift

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:27.427
all the dates for
that one patient

00:11:27.427 --> 00:11:29.880
consistently but then pick
a new random number when

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:31.150
we get to the next patient.

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:32.280
So we're going to
lose some data there.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:33.905
We're going to lose
when the dates were

00:11:33.905 --> 00:11:35.280
the same between two patients.

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:37.238
But we're going to keep
that sequence of events

00:11:37.238 --> 00:11:38.260
within a single patient.

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:42.660
So you can still do
things like duration, kind

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:44.940
of predictive based on dates.

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:48.742
But you're not going to
reveal that raw date.

00:11:48.742 --> 00:11:50.700
We talked about some of
the built-in detection.

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:54.150
Here we also allow you to go
in and augment that detection.

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:56.070
This is just a very
simple example here.

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:58.628
We're showing a little bit
of the code of what a custom

00:11:58.628 --> 00:11:59.670
detector might look like.

00:12:03.238 --> 00:12:04.780
We talked about sort
of tokenization.

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:07.300
And I want to show kind
of what that looks like.

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:09.220
You're really using
cryptography to create

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:10.492
these deterministic tokens.

00:12:10.492 --> 00:12:11.950
In this example,
we're just showing

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:13.675
the raw key that would be used.

00:12:13.675 --> 00:12:16.270
And every time we have
that key sent into the API

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:18.190
or sent into one
of the processes,

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:20.600
you're going to get the same
output for the same input

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:21.100
value.

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:23.650
So in this case, as you
see the first two rows,

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:27.210
they generate a token
that is the same.

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:29.750
And similar if you use a
method that can be reversed,

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:33.180
you can reverse those
tokens, as well.

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:36.650
And so when we apply this to
that original animation that we

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:40.010
saw, what we see down below--
and we're just kind of applying

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:42.380
different techniques
based on different types--

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:44.480
is you can start to
protect this data even

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:46.430
in the case of
unstructured data where

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:48.880
you might want to go in
and take that information,

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.850
train something on
it, or even share it

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:53.420
so people can see what's
happening in a support case.

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:55.250
But by reading that
bottom one, you

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:57.470
can't obviously pull out
and identify the person

00:12:57.470 --> 00:12:58.850
like you could in the top.

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:00.725
But you'll notice with
the credit card number

00:13:00.725 --> 00:13:02.785
even in free text,
we've tokenized it

00:13:02.785 --> 00:13:04.910
in a way-- added some
metadata that we can actually

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:08.030
reverse that if we needed to
if we had authority to the key.

00:13:10.595 --> 00:13:12.220
And so these are some
of the techniques

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:15.698
that you can apply, especially
looking at a lot of the ones

00:13:15.698 --> 00:13:17.740
you would do on these kind
of direct identifiers.

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:18.910
But how do you
know when to stop?

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:20.743
How do you know when
it's enough, especially

00:13:20.743 --> 00:13:22.535
if you're going into
a case where you might

00:13:22.535 --> 00:13:24.570
be sharing this with a
partner or exposing it

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:27.020
a little bit more broadly?

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:28.520
What else do we
need to worry about?

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:30.562
And for that, I'm going
to pass it back to Felipe

00:13:30.562 --> 00:13:33.220
and he's going to go
in a little more depth.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:34.550
FELIPE HOFFA: Thank you, Scott.

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:36.700
OK, so what do we do next?

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:41.010
We know now how to
take our private data,

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:45.910
how to take our identifiers.

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:46.830
We can match them.

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:48.960
We can do different
things with them.

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:50.273
We can bucketize it.

00:13:50.273 --> 00:13:52.690
But the question is, how do
we measure that we did enough?

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:56.070
How do we decide this is
the place where we stop?

00:13:56.070 --> 00:13:58.830
So to understand the
reidentification risk,

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:00.850
there are three basic concepts.

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:03.300
One is the identifiers,
things that we really

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:06.390
want to remove before
publishing data or sharing it--

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:08.880
a name, a credit
card, a government ID.

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:11.730
But we also have
quasi identifiers--

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:14.940
my age, where I live,
and things like that--

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:20.070
that maybe if we only keep
my age and my place of birth,

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:22.740
maybe I will be completely
anonymous in a data set.

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:27.040
Or maybe someone will see that
we have someone from Chile

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:30.720
that's a speaker at
Google, they will

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:34.450
be able to identify
me regardless of that.

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:36.030
Those are my quasi identifiers.

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:38.070
And we also have
sensitive data that

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:41.050
is what we want to protect.

00:14:41.050 --> 00:14:43.500
My health condition,
salary, something

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:49.140
that I would not like to reveal
that might be revealed if we go

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:52.770
through the quasi-identifiers.

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:55.770
So there are different
measures of how

00:14:55.770 --> 00:15:00.200
you measure how risky a data
set is for reidentification.

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:03.650
There is k-anonymity,
l-diversity, k-map, delta

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:04.320
presence.

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:06.450
I will go through
two of these now.

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:08.880
And the good thing
is that the DLP API

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:12.480
can measure all of these for--

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:14.180
give you a value
for all of these.

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:17.460
So let me show you
k-anonymity, which

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:19.470
is a property for
that asset that

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:22.890
indicates the relative
visibility of its records.

00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:26.220
And you want to measure how
many individuals you have

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:28.470
in any given group of data.

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:30.540
k equals 1 would
mean that there's

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:34.930
only one person in each
group in certain groups,

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:37.830
and that would make them
pretty identifiable.

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:40.840
To show you an example
on a public data set,

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:42.900
I'm going to take
all of the births

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:46.170
in Mexico that Mexico
has published--

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:49.770
took all of them
between 2008 and 2013.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:54.600
We have about 13 million babies
that were born in that time.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:16:00.810
And Mexico publishes a lot of
data about the babies and about

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:07.500
the mothers, including things
like where the baby was born,

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:09.570
what was the gender
of the baby, what

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:12.300
was the education of
the mother, what's

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:14.250
the civil status of the mother.

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:17.940
And what can we do with this?

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:20.430
This is a query that
I'm running DB query

00:16:20.430 --> 00:16:24.120
where I'm grouping them
by all of the babies

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:26.220
by these four variables.

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:29.310
And you can see some
groups, like the first one

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:37.110
there is that we have 405 groups
that map to only one person.

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:41.700
That would be k anonymity
equals 1 for that asset

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:43.490
because we can find
some individuals.

00:16:43.490 --> 00:16:47.220
The bigger groups from
the big various places

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:48.480
don't have this risk.

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:53.290
But there are some groups that
are really, really narrow.

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:55.950
And then we need
to decide, do we

00:16:55.950 --> 00:17:01.950
want to publish a data set
that has a k-anonymity of 1?

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:05.859
Do we want to broaden
these groups by--

00:17:05.859 --> 00:17:09.940
or do we want to
remove all of them?

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:13.802
But at least we know that there
is a k-anonymity equals 1.

00:17:13.802 --> 00:17:17.980
And then there are different
ways of measuring it.

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:20.440
I did this with a SQL query.

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:25.869
But we also bring
it in the DLP API

00:17:25.869 --> 00:17:29.060
so you can use the API to
just run a call like this.

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:36.020
And instead of wondering how
to calculate these values,

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:39.420
you can just call the LP,
ask it to-- in this case,

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:42.410
we are asking it to
calculate the k-anonymity.

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:45.740
And we're giving it the
columns, the quasi identifiers,

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:49.080
that we want to use
to measure this value.

00:17:52.290 --> 00:17:56.310
And then you get these
values straight from the API.

00:17:56.310 --> 00:17:59.360
Now, k-anonymity is a
pretty standard value

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:02.780
that a lot of the
industry, research, science

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:05.400
uses before sharing data sets.

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:06.920
But it's not enough.

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:09.050
Usually they say,
oh, you can publish

00:18:09.050 --> 00:18:14.810
that data set once you
have k-anonymity equals 5.

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:19.550
But I'll skip this
slide on how do we

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:23.990
fix this if we want to
improve k-anonymity.

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:28.010
You can use generalization,
so instead of publishing--

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:34.370
you could either remove
some of these rows,

00:18:34.370 --> 00:18:36.150
or you could generalize,
so for example,

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:39.590
instead of saying the specific
age or birth date of someone,

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:44.160
you could have larger groups of
people between 20 or 30 years

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:44.660
or so.

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:50.420
But let me look at this
example where I have--

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:55.370
if I have a k-anonymity of 5 and
I have a group of five births

00:18:55.370 --> 00:19:01.610
from post-graduate
divorcees in [NON-ENGLISH],,

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:04.430
this is something that I
could know very easily out

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:06.500
of someone.

00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:09.680
I could know when
they were born.

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:13.370
I could ask them, hey,
where were you born?

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:15.950
Was your mother-- what was
the education of your mother?

00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:18.740
What was her civil status?

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:24.640
And turns out, in this data
set I can find five women that

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:27.140
had babies around this time.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:32.810
And we could say that they are
pretty safe from [INAUDIBLE]..

00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:37.250
And even if I cannot
tell who each one is,

00:19:37.250 --> 00:19:39.230
they all have
something in common.

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:42.740
In fact, this data set has
a lot of sensitive data

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:47.800
like the health status of
the mothers at the time.

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:51.980
And all of the five
mothers have hepatitis B.

00:19:51.980 --> 00:19:56.850
And maybe they didn't
want me to know that.

00:19:56.850 --> 00:20:01.420
But even though we have
k-anonymity 5 here,

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:04.820
we are still missing diversity.

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:09.390
We would like to have more
diversity of the status.

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:13.160
So if we have different values
for hepatitis B in this group,

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:17.570
we would not be able to tell
that all of them have it.

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:21.170
And we would not be able to
tell if an individual has it.

00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:24.660
And then how do we
call this measure?

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:26.730
We call it l-diversity.

00:20:26.730 --> 00:20:30.180
And again, you can
just call the DLP API,

00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:33.320
ask it to make sure
the l-diversity.

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:38.390
And you have to give it not only
the quasi identifier columns,

00:20:38.390 --> 00:20:41.330
you also have to
set up what columns

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:43.970
do you want to measure
for sensitive attributes

00:20:43.970 --> 00:20:49.920
where you want to have more
diversity on the values.

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:54.510
And then the DLP API would
tell us, oh, l-diversity

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:56.430
for this data set is 1.

00:20:56.430 --> 00:20:59.110
You may want to change
some of these rows.

00:20:59.110 --> 00:21:00.360
You might want to delete them.

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:02.280
You might want to
generalize them, et cetera.

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:09.840
I'm not going to go through
all the measures here.

00:21:09.840 --> 00:21:12.480
k-map and delta process
are also super interesting,

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:14.670
because they are
able to incorporate

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:17.950
more statistical data
into these measures.

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:20.930
But we published a
blog post recently.

00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:26.600
And also our documentation
goes over these measures.

00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:32.830
But to remember here,
the best practices

00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:35.360
when dealing with
data privacy, first

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:37.480
you want to minimize exposure.

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:40.450
With the DLP API, you can
remove sensitive data.

00:21:40.450 --> 00:21:43.640
You can remove identifiers
if you want to remove them.

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:47.380
But you might also want
to reserve some of them

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:50.860
to keep the value, but
do some tokenization.

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:54.490
Do date shifting, do
generalization, et cetera.

00:21:54.490 --> 00:21:57.950
And to know when you are
done to determine as a group,

00:21:57.950 --> 00:22:01.300
as a team, as an
organization where to stop,

00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:05.680
you can measure the risk
of re-identification.

00:22:05.680 --> 00:22:09.370
And you can reduce that
risk by taking these steps.

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:10.910
But now it's demo time.

