00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:10.190
so let's just call
it a DLP demo.

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:14.910
DLP data flow demo.

00:28:14.910 --> 00:28:19.140
So you can see just having some
of those runtime parameters.

00:28:19.140 --> 00:28:20.890
I'm executing AETL
job that's going

00:28:20.890 --> 00:28:24.075
to do a lot of
tokenization task for me.

00:28:24.075 --> 00:28:25.200
So let's just run this job.

00:28:32.670 --> 00:28:34.585
So you also require
a temporary location.

00:28:39.270 --> 00:28:41.330
So when you're writing
it to Big Query,

00:28:41.330 --> 00:28:43.580
it requires a temporary
location in a GCS bucket.

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:53.532
So now this job is starting up.

00:28:53.532 --> 00:28:54.990
Let me just show
you-- so you could

00:28:54.990 --> 00:28:58.750
see there four different files
that I have in CSV format.

00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:00.225
And if I just do a quick count--

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:08.570
So what we're
about to do, around

00:29:08.570 --> 00:29:11.300
1.6 million rows in a CSV--

00:29:11.300 --> 00:29:12.920
four CSV files.

00:29:12.920 --> 00:29:16.160
And we're going to tokenize four
or five different phases, which

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:19.430
I'm going to walk you
guys through in a bit.

00:29:19.430 --> 00:29:22.340
But it's going to be in
Big Query table format.

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:26.850
So with that, I'll hand
it over to Patrick,

00:29:26.850 --> 00:29:29.955
who is going to walk you
through the DLP template.

00:29:29.955 --> 00:29:30.830
PATRICK LECUYER: Yes.

00:29:30.830 --> 00:29:33.830
So let's go back to the
slides for a few minutes

00:29:33.830 --> 00:29:35.510
while the job runs.

00:29:35.510 --> 00:29:39.350
So what we're doing right now--
so the job that Masus started

00:29:39.350 --> 00:29:42.920
will actually take
a data set in batch

00:29:42.920 --> 00:29:46.280
and run it line by
line through DLP API

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:49.220
to do a different number
of transformations

00:29:49.220 --> 00:29:52.460
that were described a bit
earlier today in presentation.

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:54.800
So basically, we're
starting with that data set.

00:29:54.800 --> 00:29:57.620
These are CSV
files, but we would

00:29:57.620 --> 00:30:00.050
be able to support
any type of format.

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:02.420
So we can-- this is
a very versatile,

00:30:02.420 --> 00:30:04.730
can start from any
format to any other.

00:30:04.730 --> 00:30:08.000
Some of those columns have
identifiable information

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:10.220
that needs to be de-identified.

00:30:10.220 --> 00:30:12.570
The problem that
we're trying to solve,

00:30:12.570 --> 00:30:16.760
then, is try to identify
what those columns are

00:30:16.760 --> 00:30:20.390
and how we configure DLP
API to do the right work.

00:30:20.390 --> 00:30:23.200
So we apply the right
transform on the right columns.

00:30:23.200 --> 00:30:27.500
And DLP API has a very
useful feature called the DMP

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:28.892
templates for that.

00:30:28.892 --> 00:30:31.100
And when we were working
with that financial services

00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:34.100
customer, this was something
that was key to the solution

00:30:34.100 --> 00:30:35.687
because the problem
we're trying--

00:30:35.687 --> 00:30:36.770
this is all fine and good.

00:30:36.770 --> 00:30:37.580
This is one demo.

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:38.660
We have one data set.

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:40.950
It's simple to
understand what we do.

00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:42.870
But as you increase
the number of data set

00:30:42.870 --> 00:30:46.700
and as you add the
stream data to the mix,

00:30:46.700 --> 00:30:51.050
it becomes complex to know
what needs to be applied where.

00:30:51.050 --> 00:30:55.460
DLP templates will allow you
to decouple your code that's

00:30:55.460 --> 00:30:58.700
actually doing the tokenization,
let it stay generic,

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:02.430
and put all of those transform
configurations somewhere else.

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:05.720
So what does those
template look like?

00:31:05.720 --> 00:31:09.310
Those templates are
standard JSON files,

00:31:09.310 --> 00:31:12.665
so can be sent directly
as is to the API.

00:31:12.665 --> 00:31:14.420
I know that putting
JSON on screen

00:31:14.420 --> 00:31:17.100
is not the most sexy thing, but
bear with me for a few minutes.

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:20.090
We will walk through
what that actually means.

00:31:20.090 --> 00:31:24.140
And part of the JSON
file will identify

00:31:24.140 --> 00:31:27.920
which columns transforms
needs to be applied to.

00:31:27.920 --> 00:31:30.680
We will have different
types of transforms,

00:31:30.680 --> 00:31:34.910
so two main type of templates
and then different transform.

00:31:34.910 --> 00:31:38.600
The first one here is what we
called an inspect template.

00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:42.198
The goal here is to run on a
semi-structured data-- well,

00:31:42.198 --> 00:31:43.490
unstructured data in that case.

00:31:43.490 --> 00:31:44.270
Sorry.

00:31:44.270 --> 00:31:46.020
So we have some data.

00:31:46.020 --> 00:31:47.630
In the case of our
customer, those

00:31:47.630 --> 00:31:50.220
were customer
service notes fields.

00:31:50.220 --> 00:31:51.560
So this is a free form field.

00:31:51.560 --> 00:31:54.110
People are typing data in there.

00:31:54.110 --> 00:31:57.230
Customer service people
might by mistake put

00:31:57.230 --> 00:32:00.180
some identifiable data in
there, put credit card numbers,

00:32:00.180 --> 00:32:01.850
put addresses, names, SSNs.

00:32:01.850 --> 00:32:06.320
We've seen all sorts of 60
hours in those free form fields.

00:32:06.320 --> 00:32:08.750
What an inspect
template will do is

00:32:08.750 --> 00:32:12.800
that it will run through
those free form fields

00:32:12.800 --> 00:32:17.030
and look for certain type
of data that we identified.

00:32:17.030 --> 00:32:18.720
In that case, we made a list.

00:32:18.720 --> 00:32:21.890
So we are looking for credit
card number, email addresses.

00:32:21.890 --> 00:32:26.480
There is a series of default
info types we can look for.

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:29.450
As Scott was saying earlier,
there is also some custom

00:32:29.450 --> 00:32:31.550
detectors that you could put.

00:32:31.550 --> 00:32:37.350
So that template will actually
go and look for that data.

00:32:37.350 --> 00:32:41.240
So for the column that
was linked to this,

00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:45.890
we will look for data that will
mix those info types, either

00:32:45.890 --> 00:32:49.560
the basic one, the custom ones,
or the RegEx that we have here.

00:32:49.560 --> 00:32:50.790
So that's the first step.

00:32:50.790 --> 00:32:52.280
So if we have
columns that we don't

00:32:52.280 --> 00:32:53.960
know if we need to
de-identify, that

00:32:53.960 --> 00:32:56.510
allows us to look for the data.

00:32:56.510 --> 00:32:59.130
But just looking for the
data is obviously not enough.

00:32:59.130 --> 00:33:02.870
We need to apply that
de-identification to the data.

00:33:02.870 --> 00:33:06.010
So we will do this using
de-identify template.

00:33:06.010 --> 00:33:08.940
And the example
that we have year,

00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:11.090
we will use different
types of transforms,

00:33:11.090 --> 00:33:13.400
because we have different
types of problems.

00:33:13.400 --> 00:33:16.280
In some case, you
might do bucketing.

00:33:16.280 --> 00:33:20.450
You are worried about k
anonymity or some other aspect

00:33:20.450 --> 00:33:21.590
of your data.

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:24.650
In some cases, you just
want to obfuscate the data

00:33:24.650 --> 00:33:26.030
or redact it.

00:33:26.030 --> 00:33:28.460
In some cases, you might
also need that data

00:33:28.460 --> 00:33:30.200
to be re-identifiable.

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:33.050
We had some use cases
around fraud detection

00:33:33.050 --> 00:33:36.643
where we want to run fraud
detection models on credit card

00:33:36.643 --> 00:33:38.060
numbers that have
been anonymized.

00:33:38.060 --> 00:33:40.490
We don't want to run on the
real credit card number.

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:42.770
If we find fraud, we
need to be able to get

00:33:42.770 --> 00:33:45.200
that token back to the
actual credit card number.

00:33:45.200 --> 00:33:47.930
We need to know which
customer add fraud.

00:33:47.930 --> 00:33:50.510
Otherwise, the fraud
detection model is useless.

00:33:50.510 --> 00:33:52.970
So depending on the type of
transform that we're using,

00:33:52.970 --> 00:33:57.260
we'll be able to adapt to
those different use cases.

00:33:57.260 --> 00:34:00.350
In this case, the first
example that we have

00:34:00.350 --> 00:34:02.570
is an example where
we can actually

00:34:02.570 --> 00:34:04.550
go back and
re-identify the data.

00:34:04.550 --> 00:34:06.320
So we will be using--

00:34:06.320 --> 00:34:08.449
again, we are linking
this to the column.

00:34:08.449 --> 00:34:10.460
The template will say
which column in our csv

00:34:10.460 --> 00:34:12.670
this applies to.

00:34:12.670 --> 00:34:15.409
And you have the
field user ID here.

00:34:15.409 --> 00:34:18.920
And then it will apply format
preserved encryption to it.

00:34:18.920 --> 00:34:22.130
Again, no need to go through
all of the lines of the JSON.

00:34:22.130 --> 00:34:25.340
Again, not all that
sexy on onstage.

00:34:25.340 --> 00:34:28.040
But what is important
to understand here

00:34:28.040 --> 00:34:31.192
is that we can put this
outside of our code base.

00:34:31.192 --> 00:34:33.400
So this is something-- this
is a template name that's

00:34:33.400 --> 00:34:35.770
registered with DLP API.

00:34:35.770 --> 00:34:38.139
When Masud started
his job earlier,

00:34:38.139 --> 00:34:40.510
knowing the source
data it was running in,

00:34:40.510 --> 00:34:42.520
he just pointed
to that template.

00:34:42.520 --> 00:34:44.380
That template has all
of the configurations.

00:34:44.380 --> 00:34:45.429
You see the column name.

00:34:45.429 --> 00:34:47.469
You see the type of transform.

00:34:47.469 --> 00:34:50.100
You see the keys,
the crypto keys

00:34:50.100 --> 00:34:53.960
that will be used in that
case to do the encryption.

00:34:53.960 --> 00:34:56.949
So you can really decouple
that code-- it's very generic--

00:34:56.949 --> 00:34:58.930
from the actual transform.

00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:02.900
All of the transform will add
some more type of templates.

00:35:02.900 --> 00:35:08.530
So in this case, we are running
format preserving encryption.

00:35:08.530 --> 00:35:10.510
We might have another case here.

00:35:10.510 --> 00:35:11.260
What are we doing?

00:35:11.260 --> 00:35:12.040
Same thing again.

00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:13.290
We are identifying the column.

00:35:13.290 --> 00:35:15.460
We're going, we're
configuring this.

00:35:15.460 --> 00:35:18.070
In this case, we're doing
bucketing on a number.

00:35:18.070 --> 00:35:20.350
So we talked about
bucketing earlier.

00:35:20.350 --> 00:35:21.970
So we have numbers
within the range

00:35:21.970 --> 00:35:23.800
you want to replace
this with the number

00:35:23.800 --> 00:35:26.710
at the end of the
range in that case.

00:35:26.710 --> 00:35:28.600
Simple configuration,
we're seeing

00:35:28.600 --> 00:35:31.550
what the minimum value is,
what the maximum value is,

00:35:31.550 --> 00:35:33.310
and how we replace it.

00:35:33.310 --> 00:35:41.470
So those templates are a very
useful configuration element,

00:35:41.470 --> 00:35:43.210
because you do not need--

00:35:43.210 --> 00:35:44.800
what our customer
was scared about

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:47.740
was having to write
custom pipelines

00:35:47.740 --> 00:35:49.660
for all of those data sets.

00:35:49.660 --> 00:35:53.695
And that makes onboarding a new
data set very, very complex.

00:35:53.695 --> 00:35:55.990
You do not want to have
to go out and write

00:35:55.990 --> 00:35:58.840
code every time someone wants
to send more data to analytics.

00:35:58.840 --> 00:36:02.140
You want that process to
be as seamless as possible.

00:36:02.140 --> 00:36:05.530
Using templates and using a
generic pipeline in Dataflow,

00:36:05.530 --> 00:36:07.480
you can do this at scale.

00:36:07.480 --> 00:36:10.430
You just need to have a team
that will decide which columns

00:36:10.430 --> 00:36:11.530
need to be de-identified.

00:36:11.530 --> 00:36:15.220
This is normally offloaded to
a privacy or security team.

00:36:15.220 --> 00:36:17.230
Build those templates
for new data set

00:36:17.230 --> 00:36:19.070
and your data set is onboarded.

00:36:19.070 --> 00:36:21.460
So very useful to--

00:36:21.460 --> 00:36:23.770
especially as the data set--
the number of data sets

00:36:23.770 --> 00:36:26.500
starts to scale up and
the size of the data sets

00:36:26.500 --> 00:36:28.250
starts to scale up.

00:36:28.250 --> 00:36:30.370
So at this point, I'll
go back to the demo,

00:36:30.370 --> 00:36:32.020
pass back to Masud.

00:36:32.020 --> 00:36:35.590
We ran that transform
through 1.6 million lines

00:36:35.590 --> 00:36:37.303
and we'll see the
result of that run.

00:36:37.303 --> 00:36:38.470
MASUD HASAN: Let's just see.

00:36:38.470 --> 00:36:39.850
It just completed, by the way.

00:36:39.850 --> 00:36:41.968
It took, like, few minutes.

00:36:41.968 --> 00:36:43.510
So first thing, if
you guys remember,

