00:44:26.800 --> 00:44:29.015
There's two Dory questions
I'm going to answer.

00:44:29.015 --> 00:44:31.390
And I'm going to paraphrase
them, so if I get them wrong,

00:44:31.390 --> 00:44:33.802
then just come
find us afterwards.

00:44:33.802 --> 00:44:35.260
But the first one
asked is can this

00:44:35.260 --> 00:44:37.390
be used in kind of
multi cloud or hybrid

00:44:37.390 --> 00:44:39.220
or on prem environments?

00:44:39.220 --> 00:44:40.640
The answer is yes.

00:44:40.640 --> 00:44:42.340
So when you look
at our API, there

00:44:42.340 --> 00:44:44.750
are really two modes
you can run it in.

00:44:44.750 --> 00:44:46.480
There's a streaming
content mode.

00:44:46.480 --> 00:44:48.340
And in that mode,
the data can really

00:44:48.340 --> 00:44:49.860
come from virtually anywhere.

00:44:49.860 --> 00:44:52.360
It could be on a mobile device
and maybe you're streaming it

00:44:52.360 --> 00:44:53.500
before you save it.

00:44:53.500 --> 00:44:55.010
It could be on another cloud.

00:44:55.010 --> 00:44:55.990
It could be on prem.

00:44:55.990 --> 00:44:58.600
Now, it is going to be
using our cloud to process

00:44:58.600 --> 00:45:00.580
and inspect or
de-identify the data.

00:45:00.580 --> 00:45:02.350
But that mode does
not persist the data.

00:45:02.350 --> 00:45:04.120
So the data does not
have to be in GCP.

00:45:04.120 --> 00:45:06.820
It doesn't have to be stored
in Cloud Storage or BigQuery.

00:45:06.820 --> 00:45:08.230
It's simply streamed in.

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:10.540
And the response you get
back in that API call

00:45:10.540 --> 00:45:13.210
is either your findings if
you're doing inspection,

00:45:13.210 --> 00:45:17.320
or it is your actual mast
or transformed payload.

00:45:17.320 --> 00:45:21.730
And that is on text, tabular
data, as well as images.

00:45:21.730 --> 00:45:24.260
We can mask the images, as well.

00:45:24.260 --> 00:45:25.780
The second question
was around keys.

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:27.160
I know we showed
some examples here

00:45:27.160 --> 00:45:28.452
where you actually saw the key.

00:45:28.452 --> 00:45:31.120
So probably don't
use those keys.

00:45:31.120 --> 00:45:32.800
But that was a
question around can you

00:45:32.800 --> 00:45:36.087
use something like KMS or
a Key Management System

00:45:36.087 --> 00:45:36.670
on those keys?

00:45:36.670 --> 00:45:37.580
And the answer is yes.

00:45:37.580 --> 00:45:39.413
So we actually have an
integration with KMS.

00:45:39.413 --> 00:45:42.040
The idea is that you
would wrap the key in KMS.

00:45:42.040 --> 00:45:44.170
So the thing you're
providing in the API call

00:45:44.170 --> 00:45:47.650
or in a pipeline, like in
that template, is wrapped.

00:45:47.650 --> 00:45:49.670
So if someone saw it,
somebody got access to it,

00:45:49.670 --> 00:45:52.300
they can't actually run
the key to tokenize data

00:45:52.300 --> 00:45:54.070
or de-tokenize data.

00:45:54.070 --> 00:45:56.500
They would have to have
actual access to the key.

00:45:56.500 --> 00:46:01.090
So that gives you another avenue
for auditing and access control

00:46:01.090 --> 00:46:01.930
using KMS.

00:46:01.930 --> 00:46:04.040
So that is definitely
the preferred method.

00:46:04.040 --> 00:46:05.498
But if you have
other systems where

00:46:05.498 --> 00:46:08.750
you need to stream the
key in, we do support it.

00:46:08.750 --> 00:46:11.060
And I think that was the last
one on the Dory question.

00:46:11.060 --> 00:46:11.550
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:11.550 --> 00:46:11.620
SCOTT ELLIS: OK.

00:46:11.620 --> 00:46:13.066
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:15.367 --> 00:46:17.200
SCOTT ELLIS: So the
performance implications

00:46:17.200 --> 00:46:19.760
of DLP, so it depends
a lot, obviously,

00:46:19.760 --> 00:46:22.150
on how you're using it.

00:46:22.150 --> 00:46:26.890
We have built the streaming
API to support real time

00:46:26.890 --> 00:46:27.755
environments.

00:46:27.755 --> 00:46:30.130
So again, we have some demos
we can show you at our booth

00:46:30.130 --> 00:46:33.130
if you want to stop by where
you're typing in one box

00:46:33.130 --> 00:46:35.470
and it's literally
showing up pretty much

00:46:35.470 --> 00:46:36.825
in real time in the other side.

00:46:36.825 --> 00:46:38.200
So again, it
depends a little bit

00:46:38.200 --> 00:46:40.090
on the complexity of
what you're sending in.

00:46:40.090 --> 00:46:42.060
How big it is, how much it is.

00:46:42.060 --> 00:46:44.500
But it is going to add a
little bit of processing

00:46:44.500 --> 00:46:45.880
into that pipeline.

00:46:45.880 --> 00:46:47.020
Now, if you're doing--

00:46:47.020 --> 00:46:49.510
at rest scanning is another
thing that we do support.

00:46:49.510 --> 00:46:52.240
So imagine you have your data
warehouse or your data lake,

00:46:52.240 --> 00:46:56.170
something sitting in GCS and you
wanted to sample scans or just

00:46:56.170 --> 00:46:57.560
periodic scans of that data.

00:46:57.560 --> 00:46:59.020
We also support that mode.

00:46:59.020 --> 00:47:00.760
That's our second
mode, which we kind of

00:47:00.760 --> 00:47:03.310
refer to it as a storage mode.

00:47:03.310 --> 00:47:05.303
And when you're
doing that, again,

00:47:05.303 --> 00:47:07.720
depending on how big your data
is, how you're sampling it,

00:47:07.720 --> 00:47:09.220
things like that,
but you're usually

00:47:09.220 --> 00:47:13.625
running that in kind of a
background periodic job.

00:47:13.625 --> 00:47:15.500
In the example, I think,
today with Dataflow,

00:47:15.500 --> 00:47:17.910
we were using the
streaming part of the API

00:47:17.910 --> 00:47:20.650
and using Dataflow to
parallelize everything.

00:47:20.650 --> 00:47:22.150
And so we were able
to process a lot

00:47:22.150 --> 00:47:23.472
of data in a very short window.

00:47:23.472 --> 00:47:25.180
And so that's kind of
a lot of the power.

00:47:25.180 --> 00:47:26.590
Because it's not
persisting data,

00:47:26.590 --> 00:47:29.788
because it doesn't have state,
you can parallelize the API.

00:47:29.788 --> 00:47:31.330
And at that point
you're just dealing

00:47:31.330 --> 00:47:37.310
with number of workers, total
quota, and things like that.

00:47:37.310 --> 00:47:40.070
But with that, I think we're
just about out of time.

00:47:40.070 --> 00:47:41.420
Thank you again for coming.

00:47:41.420 --> 00:47:43.060
If you have any questions,
